This means that even substituting a relatively modest N=50, the quantum state description requires eleven trillion times the dimensionality when compared to the classical case. Marvellous as computers are, they cannot feasibly store or process vectors of this size. And as N gets bigger, this only gets worse. Exponentially worse. It gets really big, really fast. Really big. Really fast. I simply must emphasise this point, because there is little in life as existentially terrifying as scaling laws. Figure~\ref{fig:f1-size-matters} draws the two facts of this section on a single axis. The curve that falls is the randomness that averages away. The curve that climbs is the size of the quantum description. They are the same growth in $N$, read in two directions.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/generated/f1-size-matters.pdf}
\caption{Two facts about scale on one axis. The blue curve, read on the left, is the fraction of a single particle's randomness that survives when N particles are averaged together: it falls as one over the square root of N, the central limit theorem at work. The orange curve, read on the right on a logarithmic scale, is the number of numbers needed to write down the quantum state of N two-level particles divided by the number needed classically: it is 2 to the power N over 2N, and doubles with every particle added. At N equals 50 the quantum description needs about eleven trillion times more numbers than the classical one. Both curves are arithmetic, not measurement. Together they are the essay's argument in one picture: what makes large systems predictable is the same growth in N that makes their quantum description unreachable.}
\label{fig:f1-size-matters}
\end{figure}

So then, how quantum is life? We may answer this with a taxonomy of scales, which Figure~\ref{fig:f2-ordering-the-scales} lays out in advance. We have already covered the quantum dependence of chemistry, but there is one more arena in which quantum matters to matter. Being made of atoms, biological tissue remains susceptible to quantum effects, and we routinely exploit this with imaging instruments. Take MRI: A strong RF pulse tips and synchronises nuclear spins. Different tissues then desynchronise at different rates, and an image is reconstructed by measuring the resulting magnetic field distortions. This relies on bona fide quantum effects, but all the action is on the instrument side. We are reading out passive responses of tissue under engineered fields, not witnessing biology harness macroscopic quantum resources to function.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/generated/f2-ordering-the-scales.pdf}
\caption{The essay's taxonomy of scale as a picture. The axis runs from a single atom to a whole organism. In blue are the places where quantum mechanics is genuinely in use but on the instrument side: spectroscopy reads molecular states, MRI reads nuclear spins in tissue. In orange are the claims that quantum effects operate inside the biology itself. Enzyme tunnelling is settled chemistry. The radical-pair compass and light harvesting are serious science whose quantum label buys nothing the chemistry does not already supply. Quantum consciousness is not a theory. From the protein scale upward (shaded), any claim must meet three tests: a mechanism demonstrated in living tissue under physiological conditions, a prediction that separates quantum dynamics from a classical surrogate, and a measured advantage over that surrogate. The lower arrow records which description actually does the work at each scale.}
\label{fig:f2-ordering-the-scales}
\end{figure}

Life is not meaningfully quantum, but it is perhaps algebraic.

\section{Algebra All The Way Up}

One of the central themes of this essay has been the importance of scale. On the scale of being, quantum is merely the root note, setting the key but not the melody. It offers no guide for how we may hope to understand emergent phenomena. How does apparently endless variety come out of primitive local rules? Why do quantitative differences tip into qualitative ones? Forget the particular modality of thought -- we are asking how a little becomes a lot. In one sense this problem can be framed as the conceptual complement to that of infinite regress, each oppositely oriented to substrate or superstructure. Even the solutions are symmetric. In our case it is not turtles all the way down, but algebra all the way up.

What do I mean? The essential problem for almost all scientific inquiry is to find the correct representation. With the right mental lens, inexplicable phenomena become inevitable. The impossible becomes trivial. This naturally motivates the search for a scale-free, universal model of representation. We have had this tool for centuries, but are only now grasping its potency. It is called linear algebra.

Recall the recipe from the second section. Describe the thing you care about as an arrow in a large space. Move it by a rule that respects straight lines. Read out what you want by projecting. I said then that quantum theory is this recipe plus two extras, and I have since spent three sections showing that the extras are exactly what scale destroys. Non-commutation averages away. The multiplying of spaces turns from a resource into a curse once there are more than a handful of parts. What is left when the extras are gone is the recipe, and the recipe was never uniquely quantum. Figure~\ref{fig:f4-the-recipe} shows it running in three very different kitchens. The rest of this section is about the other two.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/generated/f4-the-recipe.pdf}
\caption{One recipe, three kitchens. The top row is the pattern: take a messy input, lift it into a much bigger space, move it there by a straight-line rule, and read out what you want by projecting. Quantum theory follows the recipe exactly (first row) and adds two extras that make it quantum: some questions cannot be asked sharply together, and composing two systems multiplies the space rather than adding to it, which is where entanglement lives. The earlier sections of the essay show that the extras are precisely what scale destroys. The recipe itself is not uniquely quantum. A physical reservoir computer follows it with a bucket of water, a loop of light or a lattice of spins as the lifting medium (second row), and a language model follows it with vectors and a similarity-weighted mixing step (third row). What survives scale is the recipe.}
\label{fig:f4-the-recipe}
\end{figure}

This story has a beautiful circularity to it. In the mid-twentieth century neuroscientists sought functional mathematical models to describe neuronal dynamics. From this emerged cybernetics \cite{wiener1948cybernetics}, which formalised the mathematical study of goal-seeking and self-correcting behaviour. The bridge from this to machine learning came through physics, via the Hopfield network. This adapted a statistical mechanical model to a ``neural circuit'', which could be analysed with the toolkit of statistical physics. It proved significant enough to earn the 2024 Nobel prize in physics \cite{Nobel2024}, and it supplied what the earlier models had lacked: a thermodynamic grammar for saying how well a network is doing.

This has proven to be -- in grand mathematical tradition -- unreasonably effective \cite{baldassi_unreasonable_2016}.

This is exemplified by the fact its most dramatic advances have come in the past decade. The entire architecture underpinning generative AI arose not from elaboration but simplification of the underlying models \cite{vaswani2017attention}. The present frontier of transformer models consist of almost nothing but linear algebra, spiced with computationally trivial nonlinearities. Their power is derived not from conceptual complexity, but from scaling laws linking performance to model size \cite{kaplan2020scaling,hoffmann2022chinchilla}. Whether the jumps in capability that appear along the way are genuine emergence or an artefact of how we score them is still argued over \cite{wei2022emergent,schaeffer2023mirage}, but the smooth part of the story is not in dispute, and Anderson was more right than he could have known. More is different. Setting aside the question of if general intelligence can be realised in this paradigm, it has already utterly transformed our understanding of what intelligence is. Ask yourself, when was the last time we spoke seriously of Turing tests?

Why should scale help here, when three sections ago it was the enemy? Because the recipe spends dimension differently from the way quantum theory is forced to. Three plain mechanisms do the work. The first is room: a bigger space has more directions, and a problem that is hopelessly tangled in two dimensions can be separated by a flat cut once it is lifted into enough of them. The second is averaging, and it is the central limit theorem returning as a friend. In high dimension sums settle down and random projections preserve the shapes they are handed, so a crude random lift is nearly as good as a designed one. The third is thinness: real data occupy a small sliver of the big space, so a simple rule suffices to describe them once you are looking from the right side. Figure~\ref{fig:f5-lifting} shows the first two in the smallest honest example I could build. Two rings of points that no straight line can separate become separable by a straight line, with fewer than one error in a thousand, after lifting into a few dozen random directions that were never trained on anything \cite{rahimi2007random}. Nothing became cleverer. The space became bigger. That is the blessing of dimensionality, and it is the same exponential as the curse. Quantum theory has to pay for every one of its $2^N$ directions. The recipe lets you choose which directions to pay for.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/generated/f5-lifting.pdf}
\caption{The blessing of dimensionality in the smallest honest example. The task (inset) is to tell two noisy rings of points apart. No straight line through the plane can do it: a linear rule on the raw coordinates is wrong about half the time, which is chance (dashed line). The blue curve shows what happens when the same points are first lifted into D random nonlinear features, fixed in advance and never trained, and a single straight-line read-out is then fitted. With 8 features the error is a few percent, with 16 it is a fraction of a percent, and beyond 32 it sits at the noise floor. Nothing about the read-out became cleverer. The space became bigger. Means and spreads are over twenty random draws. This is a toy and makes no claim about any biological system, but it is the mechanism the essay's final section relies on, and it is the counterpart of the previous figure: the same growth in dimension that is a curse for simulating quantum systems is a resource when you choose the dimensions.}
\label{fig:f5-lifting}
\end{figure}

Above all I sense a loop is closing. The addition of a thermodynamically inspired performance metric to mathematical learning echoes back to neuroscience through the free energy principle. This argues living systems that persist far from equilibrium can be interpreted as the outcome of an analogous optimisation, and posits that thermodynamic laws make life-like order inevitable \cite{Friston2010,Friston2023,England2015,HorowitzEngland2017}. To my mind, such resonances and assonances are becoming irresistible. One might still ask how it is that meat might mimic algebraic abstraction, but again we are discovering how little is required. Reservoir computing is the lifting step of the recipe done by a physical medium: drive a bucket of water, a loop of optical fibre or a lattice of spins with your signal, let the medium's own nonlinear dynamics do the lifting, and fit nothing but a linear read-out \cite{appeltant2011single,brunner2013photonic,tanaka2019prc,mccaul_reservoir_2025}. Koopman-von Neumann (KvN) dynamics is the same idea stated as a theorem: a nonlinear process can be represented as linear evolution in a higher-dimensional space \cite{mezic2005spectral}. Ironically the KvN formalism is the classical analogue to quantum theory -- a Hilbert space theory whose only difference is that every question can be asked at once. This is the conceptual link necessary to map between physical operation and abstracted purpose. And there are signs that brains found the recipe first. The grid cells of the mammalian brain lay down a periodic code for position \cite{hafting2005grid}, networks trained to navigate rediscover the same code \cite{banino2018vector,cueva2018emergent}, and a code of the same kind organises abstract conceptual spaces in humans \cite{constantinescu2016concept}. Language models use a periodic positional code for the same reason. Viewed through this lens, biological systems may be understood as embodied computation in material substrates. I won't pretend it isn't speculative, but buy me a beer and I'll show you my working.

Fair play demands one more thing. I asked quantum biology to meet three tests. The algebraic hypothesis should face the same three, and here they are. Does the richness of a system's internal representation track its performance, as it does in every machine built on the recipe? Do interventions that would disturb quantum coherence leave a biological function alone, while interventions that change the geometry of the representation -- the connectivity, the gain, the number of parts -- change it? And do rich internal dynamics plus a simple read-out suffice to reproduce the behaviour, without an explicit computation hidden somewhere else? These are questions with answers, which is more than can be said for the alternative.

All scientific answers are provisional, because all models are wrong. But some are useful. And algebraic models have proven most useful. Given enough dimensions, algebra can represent anything. It does not offer an easy resolution to the mystery of internal experience, but it suggests that sophisticated agents are an emergent property of scale. We are perhaps the ghosts in our own machines. I therefore close on a note of indulgent speculation: with the right frame, and the right scale, there is no phenomenon which cannot be captured in the span of Hilbert space. At any rate that's what I'm telling my wife the next time she insists there is more to life than algebra.

\begin{abstract}
How quantum is life? It's not. At least not in any functional sense. Here I argue from first principles for the impossibility of any physiological scale quantum phenomena which cannot be described classically. To do so I invoke scaling and complexity arguments, and highlight their universality and utility across orders of magnitude in computational study. I discuss a taxonomy of scale for quantum effects in biology, and suggest that the very properties which would make quantum biology meaningfully distinct as a discipline would simultaneously render it intractable. Finally, I argue that a more promising frame for life is not quantum but algebraic. With examples from neuroscience, applied mathematics, machine learning and physical computing, I argue that the scale-free formalism of linear algebra has far greater utility as a tool for existential investigations on the nature of life.
\end{abstract}
